{"kind":"assert","text":"R16 prefix slice, Source and evidence assessment by checker session 44e8. Not a formal review.\n\nPacket /private/tmp/artroom-r16-prefix-review-data-20261023-v1/manifest.json (SHA-256 b6bbf9c4…28a2). The two Source copies match their rows: publication-prefix-overlap.ts 6e4d0191… (3712 B) and publication-prefix-overlap.test.ts 086ce870….\n\nSource: prefixPatternOverlap is a bounded automaton over pattern segments. I compared it with the native matcher on Main (packages/platform/src/extents.ts:137-187). It agrees on four points:\n- `**` matches zero or more names (extents.ts:172-178).\n- Any other part is matched name by name through the same matches function, so `*` and ASCII-only case folding are the native ones.\n- Brackets, braces, `?` and backslash are literal in a pattern, and are refused in a prefix.\n- A pattern that matches only the prefix directory itself is \"disjoint\", because publications are descendants.\nMalformed input and work over the bound give \"unsupported\", never \"disjoint\". Only \"disjoint\" is a proof, and the caller must treat \"possible\" and \"unsupported\" as overlap.\n\nEvidence: two compilers exit 0. Baseline: 3 of 3 passed. The control replaced the final automaton result with a one-sample check, matches(pattern, prefix + \"file.txt\"). It failed only the first case, at the intended assertion: test line 10, shelf/ against shelf/private/**, expected 'disjoint' to be 'possible'. The other two passed. This is a meaningful single control.\n\nNo required finding. Architecture, Security and Simplification: clear.\n\nFor the policy slice (nonblocking now): the rules extent always holds **/AGENTS.md and **/CLAUDE.md (extents.ts:53), and infrastructure holds **/.gitignore and **/.gitattributes (extents.ts:65). Every prefix is therefore \"possible\" against those extents. The install check cannot use disjointness alone to admit a prefix against them. Request item 3 then rests on the per-publication check, which must refuse a leaf that matches a reserved pattern inside a declared prefix, for example shelf/AGENTS.md. Name this in the policy design, and witness it."}